<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

  <PropertyGroup>
    <OutputType>WinExe</OutputType>
    <TargetFramework>net8.0-windows10.0.19041.0</TargetFramework>
    <UseWinUI>true</UseWinUI>
    <Nullable>enable</Nullable>
    <ImplicitUsings>enable</ImplicitUsings>
    <RootNamespace>Phoenix.Controls.Hub.WinUI</RootNamespace>
    <ApplicationIcon>Assets\app.ico</ApplicationIcon>
    <!--
      Explicit app.manifest declaring PerMonitorV2 DPI awareness. Without
      this the WinUI 3 process runs as DPI-unaware on >100%-scaled
      monitors and Windows bitmap-stretches the whole window, making
      every text element fuzzy app-wide (worse on QHD than 1080p
      because the upscale factor is larger). The SDK-default generated
      manifest at obj/.../Manifests/app.manifest carries only
      WinAppSDK activatable-class entries with no windowsSettings, so
      we declare them here and the SDK merges its activatable classes
      into our manifest at build time. See app.manifest for the
      detailed rationale.
    -->
    <ApplicationManifest>app.manifest</ApplicationManifest>
    <UseRidGraph>true</UseRidGraph>
    <RuntimeIdentifiers>win-x64;win-x86;win-arm64</RuntimeIdentifiers>
    <WindowsPackageType>None</WindowsPackageType>
    <EnableMsixTooling>true</EnableMsixTooling>
    <!--
      Sprint H — Phoenix.Controls.Tests now ProjectReferences this exe.
      Tests build AnyCPU, which makes the WindowsAppSDK packaging path
      (Microsoft.Build.Msix.Packaging.targets:1036) trip with "Packaged
      .NET applications with an app host exe cannot be ProcessorArchitecture
      neutral." The flag below tells the SDK that a neutral package with an
      app host is acceptable for our scenario. The release pipeline still
      builds with `-p:Platform=x64`, so production output is unaffected;
      this only relaxes the gate during Dev `dotnet build` runs where Tests
      drags Hub.WinUI in transitively.
    -->
    <AllowNeutralPackageWithAppHost>true</AllowNeutralPackageWithAppHost>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <!--
    Self-contained WinUI 3 deployment. Bundles the Windows App Runtime DLLs
    (Microsoft.WindowsAppRuntime.*, Microsoft.UI.Xaml.*) directly into
    bin/x64/<Configuration>/<TFM>/ instead of relying on a framework + DDLM
    pair registered on the user's machine. Streamers run a wide mix of
    Windows builds with partially-installed / GC'd DDLMs (the framework can
    be present at the right version yet still trip the "requires Windows
    App Runtime 1.5 (>= X)" modal because no matching DDLM is registered).
    Self-contained removes the entire dependency surface — Hub.WinUI
    launches on a vanilla Win10/11 box without any runtime install step,
    at the cost of ~30MB extra in the bin output. Post-single-HUB collapse
    Architect.WinUI / Visualist.WinUI are libraries and inherit the bundled
    runtime through Hub's bin tree; only this exe needs the flag.

    Forward-ported from master 0.9.5 (b4a4888) — the original fix landed
    directly on master per "DEV does not use the release pipeline" and
    never made it to Dev, so release/0.9.6 (built from Dev) silently
    regressed the runtime-required modal.

    Conditional on Platform=x64 because SelfContained.targets rejects
    AnyCPU outright with "The platform 'AnyCPU' is not supported for Self
    Contained mode." The release pipeline (BUILD_SUITE.bat,
    installer/build-local.ps1, .github/workflows/release.yml) explicitly
    sets -p:Platform=x64 so it picks up the bundling; day-to-day Dev
    `dotnet build` runs AnyCPU and skips it without erroring.
  -->
  <PropertyGroup Condition="'$(Platform)' == 'x64'">
    <WindowsAppSDKSelfContained>true</WindowsAppSDKSelfContained>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.WindowsAppSDK" Version="1.5.*" />
    <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.Windows.SDK.BuildTools" Version="10.0.22621.*" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <ProjectReference Include="..\Phoenix.Controls.Shared\Phoenix.Controls.Shared.csproj" />
    <ProjectReference Include="..\Phoenix.Controls.Shared.WinUI\Phoenix.Controls.Shared.WinUI.csproj" />
    <!--
      Track 4 — bridge to existing WinForms Hub runtime. Services/ wraps
      Bus / HUDServer / WS / ScriptManager / ScriptRegistry
      (Phoenix.Controls.Hub). The Shared reference above already covers the
      sub-services in Phoenix.Controls.Shared (GlobalLogger, DB,
      ConfigManager, ChatMessage, BusMessage). Track 2
      owns the rest of this csproj (WinUI 3 exe shape, WindowsAppSDK, theme
      Page link); Track 4 only added the line below.
    -->
    <ProjectReference Include="..\Phoenix.Controls.Hub\Phoenix.Controls.Hub.csproj" />
    <!--
      Single-HUB collapse (TODO.md P0 #3). Architect.WinUI and
      Visualist.WinUI flipped from WinExe to library; Hub embeds their
      MainView UserControls on pillar-tab click. The pillar separation
      invariant is preserved by *reference graph* — Architect / Visualist
      still don't reference Hub; Hub references them.
    -->
    <ProjectReference Include="..\Phoenix.Controls.Architect.WinUI\Phoenix.Controls.Architect.WinUI.csproj" />
    <ProjectReference Include="..\Phoenix.Controls.Visualist.WinUI\Phoenix.Controls.Visualist.WinUI.csproj" />
    <!--
      QC27-01 — v2 ViewerServer + WebViewer bundle. The server is a code
      dependency (HubBootstrapper constructs + starts it). The WebViewer
      project ships only static content under dist/; we reference it with
      OutputItemType="Content" so its Linked "ViewerAssets/..." tree lands
      under this exe's bin without taking a code reference on the empty
      content-only .dll.
    -->
    <ProjectReference Include="..\Phoenix.Controls.ViewerServer\Phoenix.Controls.ViewerServer.csproj" />
    <ProjectReference Include="..\Phoenix.Controls.WebViewer\Phoenix.Controls.WebViewer.csproj"
                      OutputItemType="Content"
                      ReferenceOutputAssembly="false" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <Content Include="Assets\**\*">
      <CopyToOutputDirectory>PreserveNewest</CopyToOutputDirectory>
    </Content>
    <!--
      In-app HTML documentation bundle. The offline doc pages (Node Reference
      shell + renderer, tokens, README, Changelog) ship to bin/Docs/ and are
      served to DocViewerWindow's WebView2 through the phoenix.docs virtual-host
      mapping. CopyToOutputDirectory keeps them next to the exe so the viewer
      resolves them with no network.
    -->
    <Content Include="Docs\**\*">
      <CopyToOutputDirectory>PreserveNewest</CopyToOutputDirectory>
    </Content>
  </ItemGroup>

  <!--
    Track 1 ships PhoenixDark.xaml as loose Content under Shared.WinUI (no
    PRI, since Shared.WinUI is deliberately a plain net8.0-windows lib).
    To merge it as a ResourceDictionary in this WinUI 3 app we link the
    file as a Page so it lands in this assembly's PRI under
    ms-appx:///Themes/PhoenixDark.xaml. Each WinUI 3 exe in the suite
    does the same — duplication is intentional, one PRI per app.
  -->
  <ItemGroup>
    <Page Include="..\Phoenix.Controls.Shared.WinUI\Themes\PhoenixDark.xaml" Link="Themes\PhoenixDark.xaml" />
    <Page Include="..\Phoenix.Controls.Shared.WinUI\Themes\PhoenixIcons.xaml" Link="Themes\PhoenixIcons.xaml" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <!--
    Packaged brand fonts (0.10.0 foundation). Same linked-asset pattern
    the Theme Pages above use, applied to Content: any TTF dropped into
    Shared.WinUI/Assets/Fonts/ lands in Hub.WinUI's PRI under
    `ms-appx:///Assets/Fonts/<file>.ttf`, which is exactly the URI the
    FontFamily resources in PhoenixDark.xaml use. Architect.WinUI /
    Visualist.WinUI are libraries (single-HUB-host model) and inherit
    through Hub's PRI — no per-library linking needed.

    The folder ships with only README.md in this commit; the FontFamily
    fallback chain (Segoe UI Variable / Cascadia Code / Georgia / etc.)
    renders the suite identically until Majo drops the canonical TTFs
    in. See Shared.WinUI/Assets/Fonts/README.md for filenames + sources.
  -->
  <ItemGroup>
    <Content Include="..\Phoenix.Controls.Shared.WinUI\Assets\Fonts\**\*.ttf;..\Phoenix.Controls.Shared.WinUI\Assets\Fonts\**\*.otf;..\Phoenix.Controls.Shared.WinUI\Assets\Fonts\**\*.txt"
             Link="Assets\Fonts\%(RecursiveDir)%(Filename)%(Extension)">
      <CopyToOutputDirectory>PreserveNewest</CopyToOutputDirectory>
    </Content>
  </ItemGroup>

  <!--
    Sprint E (focus-visual sweep). Hub owns its own
    Themes\FocusVisuals.xaml; the .NET SDK auto-discovers it as a <Page>
    via the implicit Page glob, so no explicit ItemGroup is needed. App.xaml
    merges it after PhoenixDark.xaml — StaticResource lookups for
    EmberPrimaryBrush / CoalCardBrush walk the parent dictionary stack at
    parse time. Per feedback_visualist_architect_chrome_independence.md, Hub
    keeps its own copy so its chrome / dialog / panel surfaces can diverge
    from the editor pillars later without dragging them with it.
  -->

  <!--
    0.10.0 redesign R2 — extern alias on Microsoft.WinUI so the chrome
    access-key Run.TextDecorations underline compiles. `TextDecorations`
    is projected as `Windows.UI.Text.TextDecorations` in BOTH
    Microsoft.WinUI.dll (the WinUI 3 typing) and Microsoft.Windows.SDK.NET.dll
    (the CsWinRT projection of WinRT), so any unqualified reference trips
    CS0433. The Target below tags the resolved Microsoft.WinUI reference
    with both `global` (keeps the implicit namespace lookup working for the
    rest of the project) and `winui` (an extern alias HubChrome.xaml.cs can
    use to disambiguate the enum). The implicit Microsoft.WinUI reference
    is pulled via the WindowsAppSDK package — we can't add a manual
    <Reference Include="Microsoft.WinUI"> with HintPath because the package
    layout varies per RID; modifying the resolved ReferencePath post
    `ResolveAssemblyReferences` is the canonical mid-SDK pattern.
  -->
  <Target Name="AddWinUIAlias" AfterTargets="ResolveAssemblyReferences">
    <ItemGroup>
      <ReferencePath Condition="'%(FileName)' == 'Microsoft.WinUI'">
        <Aliases>global,winui</Aliases>
      </ReferencePath>
    </ItemGroup>
  </Target>

  <!--
    Sprint H — NETSDK1152 lighter workaround so Phoenix.Controls.Tests can
    take a ProjectReference on this exe (needed to unit-test
    DocumentationWindow, ReadOnlyPhxWindow, App.xaml.cs dispatch logic).

    Background: this csproj's `<Content Include="Assets\**\*">` glob picks up
    `Assets/phoenix-mark.png` (the suite brand mark). Architect.WinUI — which
    we reference as a library for the pillar-tab embedding — *also* ships its
    own copy of `Assets/phoenix-mark.png` via its own
    `<Content Include="Assets\**\*">` glob. Both files land in this exe's bin
    under the same relative path `Assets\phoenix-mark.png`.

    For a standalone build the conflict resolver never fires (it's gated
    behind `_HandleFileConflictsForPublish` which only runs when a consumer
    triggers `ComputeFilesToPublish` transitively). But when Tests
    ProjectReferences this exe, the WindowsAppSDK Packaging targets pull in
    `__GetPublishItems` → `ComputeFilesToPublish`, then the SDK's
    `_HandleFileConflictsForPublish` runs `CheckForDuplicateItemMetadata` on
    `ResolvedFileToPublish` keyed by `RelativePath` and trips NETSDK1152.

    Fix: dedupe `ResolvedFileToPublish` by `RelativePath`, keeping this
    exe's own copy (matching `$(MSBuildProjectDirectory)`) and dropping
    referenced-library duplicates. Runs BeforeTargets matching the conflict
    check's AfterTargets so the dedupe wins. Branding stays intact — this
    exe's own Assets/phoenix-mark.png still copies to bin (verified by the
    Sprint H bin-listing check).

    Heavier alternative (sibling Hub.WinUI.Shared library) was explicitly
    deferred — see TODO.md Sprint H entry. This Target is the lighter
    workaround the sprint authorized.
  -->
  <Target Name="DeduplicatePhoenixBrandAssets"
          AfterTargets="ComputeFilesToPublish"
          BeforeTargets="_HandleFileConflictsForPublish">
    <ItemGroup>
      <!--
        Keep this exe's own Assets\phoenix-mark.png; drop any duplicate
        sourced from a referenced library project (Architect.WinUI's copy).
        Filter by RelativePath==Assets\phoenix-mark.png AND source path NOT
        under this project's directory.
      -->
      <ResolvedFileToPublish Remove="@(ResolvedFileToPublish)"
        Condition="'%(RelativePath)' == 'Assets\phoenix-mark.png' And !$([System.String]::new('%(FullPath)').StartsWith('$(MSBuildProjectDirectory)'))" />
    </ItemGroup>
  </Target>

</Project>
